-# ICdetection — v2.1 Roadmap
+# ICdetection — Roadmap (v2.1 shipped, v2.2 pending)
 
-**Status:** v2.0 is frozen. No code changes are planned for the next 2–3 months except genuine bug fixes.
+**Status:** v2.1 is released. The first field-collection phase (1,065 records / 59 days) is complete and has been analysed against the source code; `CHANGELOG-v2.1.md` holds the full evidence.
 
-The goal of this frozen period is to collect field data, export CSVs, study false positives, and decide future changes with real evidence instead of guessing.
+The freeze did its job, though not the way it was expected to: the most valuable result was discovering that the collection itself was broken in four ways. Those are now fixed, and a second collection phase can produce data that actually answers the remaining questions.
+
+### Shipped in v2.1 (see CHANGELOG-v2.1.md)
+
+- **Tier 1 #1 — full identity in temporal-confirmation keys.** Done.
+- **Tier 2 #6 — more mature H15 PCI logic.** Answered with data: PCI is compared only within the same carrier (ARFCN). The field evidence showed carrier aggregation affects the reported PCI exactly as it affects the ARFCN.
+- **H14 (band downgrade) false-positive control.** The new low-band cell must read strong and must not be weaker than the previous one.
+- **Forensic reason preserved for sub-threshold observations**, which is what unblocks #5 and #7 below.
+- **API tower coordinates separated from GPS positions**, with sentinel and distance validation.
+- **Periodic sampling of the serving cell**, which is what unblocks the RF fingerprint and the baselines.
+- **`ThreatProb` persisted and exported**, required before #7 is even possible.
+- Housekeeping: WiGLE `User-Agent` updated to 2.1.
+
+### Still open — and now actually answerable
+
+The items below were data-dependent and the first collection could not feed them. With v2.1's logging in place, a second 2–3 month phase should.
 
 The remaining improvement margin is not a magic new signal against sophisticated IMSI-catchers. ICdetection is limited by Android userland: it cannot inspect RRC/NAS/baseband traffic like dedicated hardware tools can. The realistic goal for v2.1 is to improve internal coherence, reduce false positives, and make better use of local history.
 
